Extract at most one source-reported Ames outcome measurement per row. Return JSON only.

Input is {"rows":[...]} with at most {{ batch_size }} rows. Each row has `id`,
`canonical_endpoint_name`, and {{ row_fields | join(", ") }}. Return every input id
once, in order:

  {"rows":[{"id":"...","status":"ok"|"relative"|"unsure"|"unavailable","measurements":[{"measurement":"<plain decimal>","unit":"<nonempty complete measurement basis>"}]}]}

Only `ok` carries exactly one measurement. Every other status carries `[]`.
An `ok` result must always have a nonempty `unit`. Judge each row independently.

`measurement_text` is the selected result field. The focal value must come from
that field, and `support_text` must tie it to the named outcome. Other visible
fields may identify the result or complete its unit, but may not donate another
value. Never select a dose, concentration, duration, timepoint, sample size,
identifier, p-value, control value, comparator value, calibration value, or
cytotoxicity readout unless it is itself the named endpoint. Do not calculate,
convert, average, or infer a value.

Use `ok` for one finite absolute point with a complete physical unit,
denominator, labeled count, or explicitly defined dimensionless metric. A
dimensionless metric still requires a nonempty measurement basis. A source-defined
ratio, fraction, percentage, correlation coefficient, partition constant, score,
index, or count can be `ok` only when the visible source text uniquely defines
what its coefficient measures.

Ratios and percentages are not automatically relative. A ratio with explicitly
named numerator and denominator, a source-defined partition ratio, or another
self-contained named metric is `ok`. A coefficient defined against an
experiment-specific control, treatment, genotype, baseline, or referent is
`relative`, including fold change, percent change or inhibition, and percent of
control. A comparison elsewhere in the row does not make an absolute focal-group
result relative.

Use `unsure` for a bound, range, conflicting or incomplete unit, several equally
focal outcomes, or conflicting source statements. A single central point with
SD, SE, or a confidence interval may remain `ok`. Use `unavailable` when the
focal outcome is absent or qualitative, or when the only numbers are conditions
or incidental statistics.

{% if has_unit_column %}For `ok`, copy a nonempty complete `unit_text` verbatim,
preserving symbols, capitalization, parentheses, analytes, scales, and
denominators. If `unit_text` is empty, use only a uniquely selected exact
contiguous physical unit or named metric span from the visible source text. If
several spans could apply, or no complete basis is stated, return `unsure`.
{% else %}This source has no separate unit column. Use only a complete unit or
named metric that is one exact contiguous span in the visible source text. If no
complete basis is stated, return `unsure`.{% endif %}

Prefer the most specific source span: for example `(R)-PEG/(S)-PEG ratio` rather
than bare `ratio`, and `Pearson correlation coefficient` rather than
`dimensionless`. Do not paraphrase, expand abbreviations, change singular or
plural, replace punctuation, use an endpoint name as the unit, or add explanatory
words. Bare `frequency`, `rate`, `score`, `index`, `count`, or `ratio` is
incomplete unless the source explicitly defines that complete metric. Never
return an empty unit, `unitless`, or `dimensionless` for `ok`.

Return the focal value as a finite plain decimal. Remove correctly grouped
thousands separators. Keep a printed power-of-ten scale in the unit: coefficient
`3.2` printed with `10^-6 events/cell` remains measurement `3.2`, unit
`10^-6 events/cell`. Never apply or reverse the scale. A string presenting `vs`,
`from ... to ...`, multiple semicolon-separated outcomes, or several equally
focal values is not one absolute point.

Additional fields and endpoint summaries identify the selected source outcome;
they cannot supply a missing value, unit, denominator, scale, or metric definition.
